<Article slug="keys-and-values">
<p>Harbour sells a blue mug. Hill sells a red bowl. Both shops happen to call their product <code>42</code>. They use the same shopping platform, whose application saves product cards in Redis under keys such as <code>product:42</code>.</p>
<p>A visitor to Harbour loads the mug, and the application saves its card. The next visitor opens Hill’s product 42. The lookup succeeds, the response is fast, and the visitor sees a blue mug. Every Redis command worked. The application asked the wrong question.</p>
<p>A cache key decides which requests may share an answer. Let’s make that decision visible. Send the first two requests below, then include the shop in the key and start again.</p>
<KeyIdentity />
<p>Adding the shop separates the mug from the bowl. Now send all six requests. The French-language card and the euro price still collide with the first saved Harbour card. Include language and currency as well, and those answers get their own slots. Requests five and six can still reuse the earlier English, sterling cards.</p>

<h2>Cache keys define which answers are interchangeable</h2>
<p>The useful rule is stronger than “give each object a unique key”. If two requests produce the same key, the saved value must be an acceptable answer to either request. That includes its meaning, its permissions and its agreed freshness. A product ID only identifies a reusable answer if everything else that affects that answer is already fixed.</p>
<p>In the illustration, the same key initially groups all six requests. There are five hits, but four return the wrong card. With shop, language and currency included, there are only two hits, and both are useful. Correctness cannot be recovered by improving the hit rate: the faulty key already has the higher one.</p>
<p>Now include <strong>Viewer</strong> too. Every request gets a separate entry, even though Ada and Eve need identical public cards. We have removed safe sharing. The key is more specific than the answer requires, so the cache stores duplicate values and does more source work.</p>
<p>This is a design exercise, not a rule to omit users from keys. If the card includes a negotiated discount, a private annotation or a permission-dependent field, the relevant user or policy context changes the answer. Sometimes the right solution is to cache the shared public part, then add personal information after the lookup. Sometimes the complete response genuinely belongs to one user. Choose the boundary before choosing the key.</p>
<p>For an application serving many organisations, each organisation is often called a <strong>tenant</strong>. An identifier unique inside a tenant is not necessarily unique across the service. Include the tenant in a shared cache’s key unless a stronger identifier already provides that separation. Derive that tenant from the application’s validated request context; blindly trusting a tenant name supplied by the caller does not establish access.</p>
<p>Cache hits must obey the same authorisation rules as misses. Check the caller’s permissions before returning protected information, regardless of where the bytes came from. A key containing a user ID is a way to locate data, not proof that the caller may read it. Nor does a once-authorised cached response remain authorised after access is revoked. The safe design depends on how permissions are checked and changed, not just on the spelling of the key.</p>

<h2>Key namespaces, encoding and schema versions</h2>
<p>Redis treats a key as bytes. Colons are a naming convention, not a directory hierarchy. <code>catalogue:product:42</code> and <code>catalogue:product:43</code> are two independent keys; Redis does not create a parent object called <code>catalogue</code>. A prefix can help teams distinguish their entries, but it does not supply isolation or access control by itself.</p>
<p>For our card, we could use a fixed prefix followed by an encoded tuple of the inputs that change the answer:</p>
<pre><code>{`card:v1:["harbour",42,"en","GBP"]
card:v1:["hill",42,"en","GBP"]
card:v1:["harbour",42,"fr","GBP"]`}</code></pre>
<p>Here <code>card</code> names the purpose, <code>v1</code> names the representation contract, and the array contains tenant, product ID, language and currency in a documented order. The browser experiment uses the same principle, with product ID first so you can see fields being added. The order is arbitrary; agreement between all readers and writers is essential.</p>
<p>Encoding is part of that agreement. Simply joining unrestricted strings with a colon can merge different inputs:</p>
<pre><code>{`tenant = "a:b", product = "c"   → a:b:c
tenant = "a",   product = "b:c" → a:b:c`}</code></pre>
<p>A fixed-order JSON array keeps those boundaries: <code>["a:b","c"]</code> differs from <code>["a","b:c"]</code>. Use one canonical encoder across the services that share the cache, with documented input types and normalisation. An integer <code>42</code> and the string <code>"42"</code> are different tuple elements. Decide whether your application considers them the same identifier before constructing the key.</p>
<p>The same issue appears with query results. A search key may need the tenant, query, filters, sort order, page and page size. Two filter objects can have the same meaning despite a different property order, so a raw JSON object serialisation may waste sharing. Conversely, sorting a list whose order is meaningful can merge different queries. Normalise according to the query’s semantics, then encode; do not let a convenient string formatter define the semantics.</p>
<p>For long inputs, a digest can shorten the encoded portion of a key. It cannot restore a field you forgot to include. It also introduces the possibility of hash collisions, so choose the digest and any stored-input verification to match the consequences of returning the wrong answer. Treat hashing as a representation choice, not an authorisation or secrecy mechanism. Keep passwords, access tokens and unnecessary personal information out of keys: keys can appear in diagnostic tools, logs and operational exports.</p>
<p>The schema version handles a different kind of change. Suppose the old value stores a decimal price in pounds, while new code expects an integer number of pence. A rolling deployment may run both readers at once. Giving the new contract a <code>v2</code> namespace prevents it from accidentally decoding a <code>v1</code> value. Old entries can expire under their existing policy. This deliberately creates a cold cache for the new version, which the rollout must accommodate.</p>
<p>A schema version does not mean the product data is fresh. <code>v2</code> describes how to interpret the answer; a product revision describes which source state produced it. Keep those ideas separate. Adding a timestamp or random value to every key avoids collisions by preventing reuse altogether.</p>

<h2>Cache granularity and dependencies</h2>
<p>So far we have saved the complete product card. We could instead save the product record and finish building the card on each request. That changes both the work we avoid and the changes that can make the saved value stale.</p>
<p>Below, the page uses a product, its category and a price from three source records. Change the category from Kitchen to Gifts, then switch between caching the whole response and caching only the product record.</p>
<Representation />
<p>When the whole response is cached, a hit can avoid all three source reads and the assembly work. But the saved response contains the category and price as well as the product. A change to any of those dependencies can make it stale.</p>
<p>When only the product record is cached, changing the category does not make that saved product record incorrect. The application reads the category and price while assembling the page, so those changes appear immediately in this simplified example. It also performs two source reads on every hit. The cache has a narrower responsibility and saves less work.</p>
<p>Real applications may batch these reads or fetch several records with one query; three records do not always imply three network round trips. The important distinction is the work and state inside the saved answer. Cache where there is worthwhile reuse, and identify everything whose change can invalidate that reuse.</p>
<p>This becomes especially important for <strong>query result caching</strong>. Suppose we save the first twenty products in the Gifts category. Editing one of those products may affect the result, but so can moving a previously absent product into Gifts. Invalidating only the IDs already present in the cached list misses that second case. A result depends on the selection and ordering rules, not merely on the rows it happened to contain last time.</p>
<p>Large, assembled responses can reduce work substantially. They can also duplicate common information across many keys and make invalidation fan out. A category name might appear in thousands of saved product cards. Smaller cached records reduce that duplication but leave more joins, lookups or rendering for the request path. Neither granularity is universally better; the workload and the freshness contract decide which work is worth keeping.</p>

<h2>Redis strings, hashes and replacement semantics</h2>
<p>The application’s cache boundary and Redis’s value type are separate choices. A Redis <strong>string</strong> is a sequence of bytes. It can contain a JSON response, an HTML fragment, a serialised object or compressed data. Redis does not interpret JSON stored with <code>SET</code>; the application encodes it before saving and decodes it after reading.</p>
<p>Saving a complete response as one string gives it one replacement boundary. A <code>GET</code> observes a whole stored string; it does not receive half the previous <code>SET</code> and half the next. That does not guarantee the response was built from a consistent source snapshot. If the application assembled mismatched source versions, Redis faithfully stores that mismatch.</p>
<p>A Redis <strong>hash</strong> holds named fields under one key. With <code>HSET</code> you can update selected fields; with <code>HGET</code> or <code>HMGET</code> you can read one or several. This can avoid transferring and decoding a large object when a request only needs a small part. It also means a field update is not a replacement of the complete object.</p>
<p>Consider a cached product with a discount. The following commands are for a disposable Redis instance:</p>
<pre><code>{`HSET demo:product:v1:42 name "Blue mug" category kitchen discount 10
HSET demo:product:v1:42 name "Blue mug" category gifts
HGET demo:product:v1:42 discount
# "10"`}</code></pre>
<p>The discount remains. The second <code>HSET</code> did not mention it, so it did not remove it. If your application treats that second command as a complete refreshed snapshot, it has accidentally retained an old field. Explicitly delete obsolete fields, or choose a replacement protocol that really replaces the snapshot. Deleting and recreating a hash with separate commands introduces an intermediate absence; a transaction or script can address Redis-local atomicity, which we will examine later.</p>
<p>Likewise, reading fields in separate commands can span an update. <code>HMGET</code> reads the requested fields in one command, but it cannot repair a writer that updates logically related fields in separate steps. Decide which fields must change together, and make the writer’s operation match that decision.</p>
<p>Expiry also belongs to the write contract. Ordinary <code>HSET</code> preserves an existing key-level expiry; it does not give a newly created hash a lifetime automatically. A plain <code>SET</code> replacement clears an old expiry unless the command supplies an expiry option or uses <code>KEEPTTL</code>. Do not assume two value representations have identical lifetime behaviour. The next article follows those lifetimes directly.</p>
<p>The <a href="/learn/databases/caching/examples/keys-and-values.sh" download>Redis keys and values worksheet</a> creates its own network-isolated Redis 8.2.2 container and removes it afterwards. With Docker running, save the script and run <code>sh keys-and-values.sh</code>. It demonstrates separate tenant values, selected hash reads, the retained-discount case, empty versus missing fields, and preserved hash expiry. All of its command assertions were run during preparation.</p>

<h2>Negative caching: saving “not found”</h2>
<p>A repeated lookup need not find a product to be expensive. A broken link may ask for product 99 thousands of times. If every miss goes to the database and the database repeatedly says “no such product”, we are repeating useful work just as surely as when it returns a mug.</p>
<p><strong>Negative caching</strong> saves a successful answer that the requested thing does not exist. Read product 99 twice below. Then create the product and read it again.</p>
<Negative />
<p>The second lookup is a cache hit even though the application’s answer is “not found”. After creation, it is still a hit, but now that answer is stale. Advance time twice or invalidate the result, then read again. The cache miss allows the application to discover the new product.</p>
<p>There are three distinct states here: no cache entry, an entry containing a found product, and an entry recording absence. Preserve all three in the representation. For example, a cache value might be either <code>{'{"kind":"found","product":{…}}'}</code> or <code>{'{"kind":"not_found"}'}</code>, while a null Redis reply means no entry. Do not use the same decoded null for both “we have no saved answer” and “we saved an answer saying nothing exists”.</p>
<p>Choose an absence lifetime from the consequences of hiding a newly created item and the cost of checking again. Creation can also trigger invalidation. A ten-second negative TTL might be harmless for one catalogue and unacceptable for a newly created document that its author expects to open immediately. An authoritative read after creation may be a better fit for that flow.</p>
<p>A timeout is not a negative result. It tells us we failed to obtain an answer, not that the product does not exist. Turning source errors into cached “not found” entries can make an outage persist in the application’s responses after the source recovers. Keep the error path distinct.</p>
<p>“Not found” can also be an access-dependent answer. If a service deliberately hides a private document from an unauthorised caller, caching that response under a globally shared document key could hide it from its owner too. Reuse the result only within the permission context where it means the same thing, while retaining the permission checks for every request.</p>
<p>Finally, negative caching helps with repeated missing IDs. It does little for a flood of unique random IDs: each request still reaches the source and may add another cache entry. The same applies to arbitrary search strings and filter combinations. Bound inputs, entry sizes and admission to the cache, and apply request limits where needed. Expiry limits how long entries live; it does not limit how many new entries can arrive in that interval.</p>

<h2>Specify the cached answer before implementing it</h2>
<p>We can now improve the product-page contract. The cached value is a public card for one tenant, product, language and currency. Its key encodes those fields unambiguously and includes the representation version. Two authorised requests may share it only when those answer-changing inputs agree. The application separately defines the allowed age and how product, category and price changes reach the cache.</p>
<p>That description gives you concrete tests. Ask for the same product ID in two tenants. Change the language and currency. Read the same public card as another viewer. Refresh an object after removing a field. Create a previously missing object. If the cache changes the meaning of any response beyond the agreed freshness policy, something in the key, value or update protocol is incomplete.</p>
<p>We have deliberately held capacity and most timing constant. A correctly identified answer still cannot remain useful forever or occupy unlimited memory. <a href="/learn/databases/caching/#collection">The collection continues with expiration and eviction</a>: two different reasons a saved entry stops being available.</p>

